{
  "id": "introduction-to-microservices-introduction-to-microservices",
  "slug": "introduction-to-microservices",
  "title": "Introduction to Microservices",
  "category": "Spring Framework",
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "monolithic-applications",
      "heading": "Monolithic Applications",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [],
      "wordCount": 0,
      "subsections": [
        {
          "id": "definition",
          "heading": "Definition",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A Monolithic application is a software architecture where the entire application codebase is packaged and deployed as a single unit — typically a single WAR (Web Archive) or JAR (Java Archive) file."
            },
            {
              "type": "image",
              "src": "/Users/luffy/Desktop/Computer Science/Infosys/Spring Microservices/Introduction to Microservices/Introduction%20to%20Microservices/image.png",
              "alt": "image.png",
              "originalPath": "Introduction%20to%20Microservices/image.png"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "All layers are tightly coupled and deployed together."
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 40,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "key-characteristics",
          "heading": "Key Characteristics",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "Entire functionality in a single process.",
                "May consume APIs of other applications and expose services.",
                "Simple deployment — one file to deploy."
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 22,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "examples",
          "heading": "Examples",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "Early versions of Twitter, Facebook, and Amazon started as monolithic apps."
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 11,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "advantages",
          "heading": "Advantages",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "Easier to get the big picture since all code is in one place.",
                "Simple deployment: only one WAR/JAR file.",
                "Straightforward development.",
                "Less dependency on network latency or security because all components are internal."
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 33,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "disadvantages",
          "heading": "Disadvantages",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "Deployment: Small changes require redeploying the whole app, increasing downtime.",
                "Scalability: To scale one part, the entire app must be scaled.",
                "Complexity: Large codebases become intimidating and hard to maintain.",
                "Technology Limitations: Difficult to incorporate new technologies or languages.",
                "Startup Time: Larger apps take longer to start."
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 47,
          "subsections": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "microservices-architecture",
      "heading": "Microservices Architecture",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [],
      "wordCount": 0,
      "subsections": [
        {
          "id": "definition",
          "heading": "Definition",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Microservices is an architectural style in which an application is broken down into small, loosely coupled services that communicate via lightweight protocols such as HTTP/REST."
            },
            {
              "type": "image",
              "src": "/Users/luffy/Desktop/Computer Science/Infosys/Spring Microservices/Introduction to Microservices/Introduction%20to%20Microservices/image%201.png",
              "alt": "image.png",
              "originalPath": "Introduction%20to%20Microservices/image%201.png"
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 25,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "characteristics",
          "heading": "Characteristics",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "Individually Developed: Microservices can be developed independently without waiting for others.",
                "Individually Deployed: Each service can be deployed on its own (e.g., in Docker containers).",
                "Individually Maintained: Changes in one service don’t affect others.",
                "Individually Scaled: Only the services with higher load are scaled.",
                "Communication: Lightweight protocols like REST or messaging queues.",
                "Individually Monitored: Performance and errors are tracked per service.",
                "Organized Around Business Capabilities: Services represent specific business functions rather than technical layers.",
                "Dumb Pipes, Smart Endpoints: Communication channels are simple, intelligence is in the services.",
                "Decentralized Databases: Each microservice can have its own database.",
                "Supports DevOps: Teams own the entire lifecycle — develop, deploy, maintain."
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 107,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "examples",
          "heading": "Examples",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "Netflix, Amazon, Uber evolved from monoliths to microservices to handle scaling and complexity better."
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 14,
          "subsections": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "comparison-monolithic-vs-microservices",
      "heading": "Comparison: Monolithic vs Microservices",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "image",
          "src": "/Users/luffy/Desktop/Computer Science/Infosys/Spring Microservices/Introduction to Microservices/Introduction%20to%20Microservices/image%202.png",
          "alt": "image.png",
          "originalPath": "Introduction%20to%20Microservices/image%202.png"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Aspect",
            "Monolithic",
            "Microservices"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Deployment Unit",
              "Single WAR/JAR file",
              "Multiple independently deployable services"
            ],
            [
              "Scalability",
              "Scale entire app",
              "Scale individual services"
            ],
            [
              "Technology Stack",
              "One technology stack",
              "Polyglot (each service can use different tech)"
            ],
            [
              "Development",
              "One large team, tightly coupled",
              "Multiple small teams, loosely coupled"
            ],
            [
              "Maintenance",
              "Harder as codebase grows",
              "Easier, smaller codebase per service"
            ],
            [
              "Communication",
              "Internal method calls",
              "Network calls over REST, messaging"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ],
      "wordCount": 61,
      "subsections": []
    },
    {
      "id": "service-oriented-architecture-vs-microservices",
      "heading": "Service-Oriented Architecture vs Microservices",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [],
      "wordCount": 0,
      "subsections": [
        {
          "id": "definitions",
          "heading": "Definitions",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "SOA (Service-Oriented Architecture): A design pattern where software components provide services to other components via a communication protocol, often using an Enterprise Service Bus (ESB).",
                "Microservices: A fine-grained subset of SOA with simpler communication and decentralized governance."
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 37,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "similarities",
          "heading": "Similarities",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "Both aim to break down monolithic applications.",
                "Both use service endpoints for communication.",
                "Both focus on modularizing applications into smaller components."
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 21,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "differences",
          "heading": "Differences",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "Aspect",
                "SOA",
                "Microservices"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "Communication",
                  "Complex protocols like SOAP, ESB",
                  "Lightweight protocols like REST, AMQP"
                ],
                [
                  "Security",
                  "SOAP with complex security standards",
                  "Modern techniques like OAuth2, circuit breakers"
                ],
                [
                  "Complexity",
                  "Complex orchestration with strong contracts",
                  "Simpler contracts, decentralized"
                ],
                [
                  "Implementation",
                  "Vendor-dependent, complex",
                  "Flexible, independent services"
                ],
                [
                  "DevOps Support",
                  "Development and maintenance often separated",
                  "Full lifecycle ownership by teams"
                ]
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 53,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "why-microservices-are-trending",
          "heading": "Why Microservices are Trending",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "SOA faced issues with complexity and tool support.",
                "Microservices benefit from modern tooling and cloud infrastructure.",
                "Seen as “SOA done right.”"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 21,
          "subsections": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cloud-native-applications",
      "heading": "Cloud Native Applications",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [],
      "wordCount": 0,
      "subsections": [
        {
          "id": "definition",
          "heading": "Definition",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Applications designed specifically to leverage cloud computing features such as elasticity, resilience, and scalability. Simply moving an app to the cloud does not make it cloud-native."
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 26,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "characteristics-of-cloud-native-apps",
          "heading": "Characteristics of Cloud Native Apps",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "Automatically adapt to changes in the cloud environment (e.g., scaling, failures).",
                "Use multiple instances and load balancing.",
                "Resilient to service failures.",
                "Exploit cloud features like security, monitoring, and elasticity."
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 29,
          "subsections": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "challenges-in-cloud-native-microservices",
      "heading": "Challenges in Cloud-Native Microservices",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            "Service Discovery: How one microservice finds others (since hosts/ports change).",
            "Configuration Management: Avoid duplicating common config across services.",
            "Load Balancing: Distributing traffic evenly across multiple instances.",
            "Resilience: Handling failures gracefully (fallbacks, retries).",
            "Distributed Tracing: Tracking requests across multiple microservices."
          ]
        }
      ],
      "wordCount": 39,
      "subsections": []
    },
    {
      "id": "tools-and-frameworks",
      "heading": "Tools and Frameworks",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            "Netflix OSS: Open-source solutions from Netflix for building and operating microservices (e.g., Eureka for discovery, Hystrix for resilience).",
            "Spring Cloud: Framework that simplifies using Netflix OSS and other tools with Spring Boot.",
            "Others include: Vert.x, Restlet, Akka, Ninja."
          ]
        }
      ],
      "wordCount": 38,
      "subsections": [
        {
          "id": "spring-cloud-projects",
          "heading": "Spring Cloud Projects",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "Spring Cloud Config: Centralized configuration.",
                "Spring Cloud Netflix: Integrations with Netflix OSS.",
                "Spring Cloud Security: Security features.",
                "Spring Cloud Sleuth: Distributed tracing."
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 22,
          "subsections": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "summary",
      "heading": "Summary",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            "Monolithic apps are simpler but suffer from scaling and deployment problems.",
            "Microservices break down applications into small, independently deployable services that communicate via lightweight protocols.",
            "SOA is an older architecture style similar to microservices but more complex.",
            "Microservices benefit from modern tools, cloud infrastructure, and DevOps practices.",
            "Cloud native apps are designed to take full advantage of cloud environments, beyond just hosting.",
            "Frameworks like Spring Cloud and tools like Netflix OSS provide solutions to common microservice challenges."
          ]
        }
      ],
      "wordCount": 77,
      "subsections": []
    }
  ],
